1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,148
You can calculate pi without a single
circle,

2
00:00:03,148 --> 00:00:05,946
just by dropping needles onto a wooden
floor,

3
00:00:05,946 --> 00:00:09,926
and one mathematician who tried it got six
decimal places right,

4
00:00:09,926 --> 00:00:14,154
but the numbers he wrote down show that he
almost certainly cheated.

5
00:00:14,604 --> 00:00:15,845
So before we catch him,

6
00:00:15,845 --> 00:00:19,300
let's see how a falling needle can know
anything about a circle.

7
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:22,440
With needles as long as the boards are
wide,

8
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:25,008
one lying across them always hits a crack,

9
00:00:25,008 --> 00:00:27,759
while one lying along them almost never
does.

10
00:00:28,209 --> 00:00:31,454
And since it can point any way around a
full circle,

11
00:00:31,454 --> 00:00:34,449
the chance of a crossing is exactly two
over pi,

12
00:00:34,449 --> 00:00:38,131
so twice the drops over the crossings
slowly turns into pi.

13
00:00:38,581 --> 00:00:41,759
But it's slow, because after 3,000 drops,

14
00:00:41,759 --> 00:00:45,319
an honest run usually gets only one
decimal place right.

15
00:00:45,769 --> 00:00:47,990
And that's what gives Lazzarini away,

16
00:00:47,990 --> 00:00:52,793
because his answer is exactly 355 over
113,

17
00:00:52,793 --> 00:00:55,854
a fraction for pi that's over a thousand
years old,

18
00:00:55,854 --> 00:01:01,137
and his 3,408 drops are just the right
number to land on it.

19
00:01:01,587 --> 00:01:06,143
So he most likely stopped the moment he
hit it, which means the trick is real,

20
00:01:06,143 --> 00:01:08,188
but his six decimal places weren't.
